WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.205
Book Three, Proposition Six.

2
00:00:02.205 --> 00:00:06.605
If two circles touch one another, they will not have the same center.

3
00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:11.004
Let the two circles A B C and C D E touch one another at the point C.

4
00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:13.810
I say that they do not have the same center.

5
00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.548
For, if possible, let their common center be F.

6
00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:27.080
Join F C by Postulate One, and draw the straight line F E B through at random, meeting the inner circle at E and the outer circle at B.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:33.797
Because F is the center of the circle A B C, the radius F C equals the radius F B.

8
00:00:33.797 --> 00:00:40.631
Again, because F is the center of the circle C D E, the radius F C equals the radius F E.

9
00:00:40.631 --> 00:00:50.153
Since F C equals both F B and F E, F E equals F B—the part equal to the whole, the less to the greater, which is impossible.

10
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:59.060
Therefore, F is not the common center of the circles A B C and C D E; touching circles cannot share the same center.

11
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.980
As was required to prove.

12
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:10.908
As Sir Thomas Heath notes, English editions often restricted this proposition to circles touching internally, because Euclid's diagram depicts internal contact.

13
00:01:10.908 --> 00:01:19.050
In fact, Euclid deliberately chose the more subtle internal case for demonstration, leaving the self-evident external case to the reader.

14
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:32.480
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one-hundred-four rests directly on the fundamental definitions of circle, center, and tangency—demonstrating by pure reductio ad absurdum that any two tangent circles must have distinct centers.

15
